<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joy to the World</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words published <a href="/years/1719">1719</a>; present tune published by Lowell Mason <a href="/years/1836">1836</a>, in today's arrangement <a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Joy to the World&quot; was written by the English minister and hymn writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-watts">Isaac Watts</a> as a Christian reading of Psalm 98, with an allusion to Genesis 3. It was first published in 1719, under the title &quot;The Messiah's Coming and Kingdom&quot;, in Watts's collection The Psalms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>: Imitated in the Language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, and applied to the Christian State and Worship.</p></div>
